# Heads up on pi's extension refactor

People of pi.

I wanted to give you a heads up about the direction I'm currently taking pi via the ongoing refactor. I won't give an ETA, because it's a lot of work. But I want to give you an opportunity to chime in on what's planned before it's too late.

# Current modes

pi currently supports 4 modes:

- **interactive mode**: the TUI you get when you run `pi`
- **print/json mode**: what you get when you run `pi -p` or `pi --mode json`, single-shot prompt
- **rpc mode**: what you get when you run `pi --mode rpc`
- **sdk mode**: programmatic creation and mutation of sessions in TypeScript

# Current extension API and abilities

All 4 modes can load extensions, and other resources like skills, prompt templates, or themes, either from disk or provided programmatically.

Extensions are basically factory functions that receive an `ExtensionApi` object on which they can register:

- event handlers
- custom tools, including custom call and result renderers
- custom slash commands
- custom keyboard shortcut handlers
- custom CLI flags
- custom providers
- custom message renderers

That same API also has methods to mutate the session, for example model, thinking level, session name, labels, active tools, and appended entries, and submit user-like prompts that either trigger a turn immediately or are queued to execute during or after the current turn.

Event handlers, custom tools, and custom shortcut handlers receive an `ExtensionContext` allowing them to do read-only operations on the session. That context also contains an `ExtensionUIContext`, so these handlers can render fully custom UI in interactive mode, such as overlays, components temporarily replacing the editor, status line, widgets above and below the editor, and so on.

Custom slash command handlers receive an `ExtensionCommandContext`, which adds write operations for session mutation.

The repository has a ton of example extensions. E.g. have a look at the simple custom question tool extension. It defines a custom tool that, when invoked by the agent, brings up a custom UI component via `ctx.ui.custom`, showing the questions the agent wants the user to answer. The custom UI component temporarily replaces the editor in the TUI until the user submits an answer or cancels via Escape. The tool call and result also have custom rendering, defined on the custom tool via `renderCall` and `renderResult`.

Extension business logic and UI are intermingled. That way, extensions can be pretty compact and self-contained.

Extensions are loaded as ESM modules into the same Node process the session and interactive-mode TUI are running in. This allows extensions to directly exchange live JavaScript objects with the TUI and agent loop.

# The problem with extensions and client/server mode

In a client/server architecture, the server is headless and all about business logic and managing the session, while the client renders the current session state and surrounding UI.

Our current extensions mix business logic and UI code. That is fundamentally incompatible with the client/server architecture. There is also no sane way to make existing intermingled extension code magically run business logic code on the server and UI code on the client.

And even if we could somehow make it work, we would still be tying extensions to one specific rendering interface: the TUI.

In my view, I want to be able to have multiple rendering interfaces, at a minimum TUI and some kind of web stack, browser, Electron, Electrobun, whatever.

This requires a clean split between an extension's business logic and its UI.

# The new extension setup

The new extension setup splits extension business logic from extension UI.

At the conceptual level, an extension can now have:

- a **server** part
- a **TUI client** part
- a **web client** part, still somewhat TBD

The server part owns the actual extension logic:

- registering tools
- subscribing to events
- mutating session state
- driving agent behavior
- requesting UI interactions in an abstract way

The client parts own rendering and local interaction details for a specific frontend.

That means the same extension can have one implementation of the business logic, while exposing different UI for:

- the TUI
- a web-stack based client
- potentially other frontends later, though I think web-stack is all we'll ever need

The server should not know or care whether the user is interacting through a terminal, a browser, an Electron app, or a native app. It should just request mounting a UI component by name, pass JSON-serializable data, optionally send follow-up events to that mounted component, and optionally wait for the component to return a result and get unmounted. Some components might also be fire-and-forget and stay in the UI, just like overlays in the current pi TUI.

Ideally pi can support legacy extensions in local mode, that is no server/client split, while new extensions work against the client/server setup.

I'm not 100% sure I can continue supporting legacy extensions, but I'll do my best to make it work.

# A concrete example

Today, a tiny extension that asks the user for their name might look like this:

```ts
import type { ExtensionAPI } from "@mariozechner/pi-coding-agent";
import {
  Input,
  Text,
  truncateToWidth,
  type Focusable,
} from "@mariozechner/pi-tui";
import { Type } from "@sinclair/typebox";

class AskNameComponent implements Focusable {
  private input = new Input();
  private _focused = false;

  get focused(): boolean {
    return this._focused;
  }

  set focused(value: boolean) {
    this._focused = value;
    this.input.focused = value;
  }

  constructor(
    private prompt: string,
    private close: (result: string | null) => void,
  ) {
    this.input.onSubmit = (value) => this.close(value.trim() || null);
    this.input.onEscape = () => this.close(null);
  }

  render(width: number): string[] {
    return [truncateToWidth(this.prompt, width), ...this.input.render(width)];
  }

  handleInput(data: string): void {
    this.input.handleInput(data);
  }

  invalidate(): void {
    this.input.invalidate();
  }
}

export default function (pi: ExtensionAPI) {
  pi.registerTool({
    name: "ask_name",
    description: "Ask the user for their name",
    parameters: Type.Object({}),
    async execute(_id, _params, _signal, _onUpdate, ctx) {
      const name = await ctx.ui.custom<string | null>(
        (_tui, _theme, _kb, close) => {
          return new AskNameComponent("What is your name?", close);
        },
      );

      return {
        content: [
          { type: "text", text: name ? `User name: ${name}` : "Cancelled" },
        ],
        details: { name },
      };
    },
    renderCall() {
      return new Text("ask_name", 0, 0);
    },
    renderResult(result) {
      return new Text(String(result.details?.name ?? "Cancelled"), 0, 0);
    },
  });
}
```

In the new model, the same extension starts out split.

`ask-name/manifest.ts`

```ts
export const manifest = {
  id: "ask-name",
  api: "1.0",
};
```

`ask-name/server.ts`

```ts
import type { ServerExtensionAPI } from "@pi/server";
import { Type } from "@sinclair/typebox";

export function server(pi: ServerExtensionAPI) {
  pi.registerTool({
    name: "ask_name",
    description: "Ask the user for their name",
    parameters: Type.Object({}),
    async execute(_id, _params, signal, _onUpdate, ctx) {
      const handle = ctx.ui.open<string | null>("ask-name", {
        prompt: "What is your name?",
      });
      signal?.addEventListener("abort", () => handle.close(null));
      const { result: name } = await handle.closed;

      return {
        content: [
          { type: "text", text: name ? `User name: ${name}` : "Cancelled" },
        ],
        details: { name },
      };
    },
  });
}
```

`ask-name/client.tui.ts`

```ts
import type { TuiExtensionAPI } from "@pi/client-tui";
import {
  Input,
  Text,
  truncateToWidth,
  type Focusable,
} from "@mariozechner/pi-tui";

class AskNameComponent implements Focusable {
  private input = new Input();
  private _focused = false;

  get focused(): boolean {
    return this._focused;
  }

  set focused(value: boolean) {
    this._focused = value;
    this.input.focused = value;
  }

  constructor(
    private prompt: string,
    private close: (result: string | null) => void,
  ) {
    this.input.onSubmit = (value) => this.close(value.trim() || null);
    this.input.onEscape = () => this.close(null);
  }

  render(width: number): string[] {
    return [truncateToWidth(this.prompt, width), ...this.input.render(width)];
  }

  handleInput(data: string): void {
    this.input.handleInput(data);
  }

  invalidate(): void {
    this.input.invalidate();
  }
}

export function tui(ui: TuiExtensionAPI) {
  ui.registerComponent("ask-name", (data, _tui, _theme, _kb, close) => {
    return new AskNameComponent(data.prompt, close);
  });

  ui.renderToolCall("ask_name", () => new Text("ask_name", 0, 0));
  ui.renderToolResult(
    "ask_name",
    (result) => new Text(String(result.details?.name ?? "Cancelled"), 0, 0),
  );
}
```

The manifest belongs to the extension, not to the server or client side specifically. The server owns the tool. The client owns the UI. The important bit is that `close(result)` passes the final result back to the server-side tool. I left out some sugar to make this a bit more type-safe for brevity.

## Single-file new-style extensions

For extensions that only want to support TUI but not web, you can also pack everything into a single file, like we do in legacy extensions.

`ask-name.ts`

```ts
import type { ServerExtensionAPI } from "@pi/server";
import type { TuiExtensionAPI } from "@pi/client-tui";
import {
  Input,
  Text,
  truncateToWidth,
  type Focusable,
} from "@mariozechner/pi-tui";
import { Type } from "@sinclair/typebox";

export const manifest = {
  id: "ask-name",
  api: "1.0",
};

class AskNameComponent implements Focusable {
  private input = new Input();
  private _focused = false;

  get focused(): boolean {
    return this._focused;
  }

  set focused(value: boolean) {
    this._focused = value;
    this.input.focused = value;
  }

  constructor(
    private prompt: string,
    private close: (result: string | null) => void,
  ) {
    this.input.onSubmit = (value) => this.close(value.trim() || null);
    this.input.onEscape = () => this.close(null);
  }

  render(width: number): string[] {
    return [truncateToWidth(this.prompt, width), ...this.input.render(width)];
  }

  handleInput(data: string): void {
    this.input.handleInput(data);
  }

  invalidate(): void {
    this.input.invalidate();
  }
}

export function server(pi: ServerExtensionAPI) {
  pi.registerTool({
    name: "ask_name",
    description: "Ask the user for their name",
    parameters: Type.Object({}),
    async execute(_id, _params, signal, _onUpdate, ctx) {
      const handle = ctx.ui.open<string | null>("ask-name", {
        prompt: "What is your name?",
      });
      signal?.addEventListener("abort", () => handle.close(null));
      const { result: name } = await handle.closed;

      return {
        content: [
          { type: "text", text: name ? `User name: ${name}` : "Cancelled" },
        ],
        details: { name },
      };
    },
  });
}

export function tui(ui: TuiExtensionAPI) {
  ui.registerComponent("ask-name", (data, _tui, _theme, _kb, close) => {
    return new AskNameComponent(data.prompt, close);
  });

  ui.renderToolCall("ask_name", () => new Text("ask_name", 0, 0));
  ui.renderToolResult(
    "ask_name",
    (result) => new Text(String(result.details?.name ?? "Cancelled"), 0, 0),
  );
}
```

That is mostly just the split example pasted into one file.

There are a couple of caveats.

First, pi should still treat the server and client parts as logically separate runtimes. In local mode both factories are executed from the same module. In client/server mode, the server factory runs on the server and the TUI factory runs on the client.

Second, extension authors should not rely on shared mutable globals between `server()` and `tui()`. If both exports close over the same top-level mutable state, local mode may appear to work while client/server mode behaves differently.

So the safe rule is simple: shared code is fine, shared mutable runtime state is not.

# Extension API versioning

New-style extensions export an API version in their manifest:

```ts
export const manifest = {
  id: "ask-name",
  api: "1.0",
};
```

pi's extension API version is just `major.minor`.

The rules are simple:

- bump **minor** for additive changes and fixes
- bump **major** for breaking changes

Compatibility is also simple:

- host major must equal extension major
- host minor must be greater than or equal to extension minor

Examples:

- pi API 1.4 loads extension 1.0
- pi API 1.4 loads extension 1.2
- pi API 1.4 does not load extension 1.5
- pi API 1.4 does not load extension 2.0

This versioning scheme only applies to new-style extensions. Extensions without a manifest are treated as legacy extensions.

# Legacy extensions

If I manage to keep legacy extensions working, they will only work in local mode.

If I fail at that, pi will fail them at load time and tell you to go bother the extension author.

# Client-side extension resolution

## TUI client, same machine

The server can send absolute resolved paths for client entrypoints. The client loads them directly. This works regardless of whether the client's cwd matches the session.

The client side must not assume its process' cwd is the session cwd. All cwd-related operations should go through the server side of the extension, which knows about the session, the file system, and all other storage. Since TUI clients are also Node apps, you need to be careful not to do any direct file operations in client code. The client is strictly a presentation layer. The server is what touches the file system.

## TUI client, different machine

A remote TUI client should not silently auto-execute server-provided Node code. But it does not have to just fail either.

If the client connects to a session and cannot resolve a required extension locally, it can prompt the user to install it. Installation could work a few ways:

- if the extension comes from an npm or git package, install that package locally, even if only temporarily
- if the extension is just a bunch of files on the server, sync those over, though this gets tricky with dependencies

The client-side installation should be low friction. Ideally the user sees something like "Session uses extension X, install it?" and can confirm with one keystroke.

If a client still cannot resolve an extension after that, custom components should fail with a structured error that includes the extension id and component name. Custom tool renderers can fall back to built-in rendering. Ambient UI like status items or widgets can just be skipped.

## Web client

Web clients are different from remote TUI clients.

pi should come with a web harness that exposes a `WebClientApi` to the extension's web entry module, just like the TUI harness exposes a `TuiExtensionApi` to `client.tui.ts`.

The server should not try to bundle the extension's web code. It cannot anticipate what web stack the extension uses. Instead, the extension author is responsible for producing browser-ready artifacts. The server just serves a directory of static files per extension.

A web-capable extension would look like this on disk:

```
ask-name/
  manifest.ts
  server.ts
  client.tui.ts
  web/
    client.web.js      ← pre-built browser entry module
    styles.css          ← optional
    worker.wasm         ← optional
```

The server serves everything under `web/` at a known URL prefix like `/extensions/{id}/...`. The web client fetches extension metadata from the server, gets the entry module URL for each extension, loads it, and calls its `web()` factory.

That is a sane trust model for the web. Browsers already expect to load code over HTTP. The entry module can reference sibling assets like CSS, WASM, or images via relative URLs because the server serves the whole directory.

For simple extensions that are just one JS file with no extra assets, the `web/` directory just contains a single `client.web.js`.

The web client API mirrors the TUI client API but uses DOM elements instead of TUI components:

`ask-name/web/client.web.ts` (before bundling)

```ts
import type { WebExtensionAPI } from "@pi/client-web";

export function web(ui: WebExtensionAPI) {
  ui.registerComponent("ask-name", (data, close, send, onEvent) => {
    const container = document.createElement("div");
    const label = document.createElement("label");
    label.textContent = data.prompt;
    const input = document.createElement("input");
    input.type = "text";
    const submit = document.createElement("button");
    submit.textContent = "Submit";

    submit.addEventListener("click", () => {
      close(input.value.trim() || null);
    });

    input.addEventListener("keydown", (e) => {
      if (e.key === "Enter") close(input.value.trim() || null);
      if (e.key === "Escape") close(null);
    });

    container.append(label, input, submit);

    return {
      element: container,
      dispose() {},
    };
  });

  ui.renderToolCall("ask_name", (args) => {
    const el = document.createElement("span");
    el.textContent = "ask_name";
    return el;
  });

  ui.renderToolResult("ask_name", (result) => {
    const el = document.createElement("span");
    el.textContent = String(result.details?.name ?? "Cancelled");
    return el;
  });
}
```

The web harness mounts the returned `element` into the UI and calls `dispose()` when the handle closes. The `close`, `send`, and `onEvent` callbacks work the same way as on the TUI side.

# The `open()` primitive

`open()` is the only primitive we need for custom extension UI. It gives you a handle-scoped bidirectional event channel between the server and any number of clients.

The core idea is simple. The server opens a named UI component by calling `ctx.ui.open()`. That creates one logical handle on the server and broadcasts it to the connected clients. Each client gets its own local instance of that component for the same handle. From that point on, both sides can exchange arbitrary events through the handle, and either side can close it.

pi does not impose any state model on top of this. The extension decides what events mean, how state is stored on the server, and how state is represented on the client. The handle is just a scoped communication channel with lifecycle.

## Server-side API

```ts
const handle = ctx.ui.open("component-name", initialData);
```

The handle exposes:

```ts
type ConnectedClient = {
  id: string;
  type: "tui" | "web" | string;
  label?: string;
};

type UiHandle<Result> = {
  send(event: unknown, options?: { to?: string | string[] }): void;
  onEvent(
    handler: (event: unknown, client: ConnectedClient) => void,
  ): () => void;

  getClients(): ConnectedClient[];
  onClientJoin(handler: (client: ConnectedClient) => void): () => void;
  onClientLeave(handler: (client: ConnectedClient) => void): () => void;

  close(result?: Result): void;
  closed: Promise<{
    result?: Result;
    client?: ConnectedClient;
    reason: "client_closed" | "server_closed" | "no_clients";
  }>;
};
```

- **`send`** sends an event to all attached clients, or to a specific client via `{ to: client.id }`
- **`onEvent`** receives events sent by clients, including which client sent it
- **`getClients`** returns the clients currently attached to this handle
- **`onClientJoin`** fires when a new client gets attached to a still-open handle
- **`onClientLeave`** fires when an attached client disconnects or detaches
- **`close`** closes the handle from the server side, with an optional final result
- **`closed`** a promise that resolves once the handle is terminally closed, including which client closed it if applicable

## Client-side API

When the client receives an `open`, it looks up the registered component by name and calls its factory. The factory receives the initial data plus three callbacks:

- **`close(result?)`** closes the handle from the client side, with an optional final result
- **`send(event)`** sends an event to the server
- **`onEvent(handler)`** receives events sent by the server

## Handles are durable session state

A running server-side session keeps a registry of all currently open handles. This matters because clients can connect and disconnect at any time.

When a new client connects to a session that already has open handles, the server replays those handles to the new client. The client gets a `ui:open` for each one and mounts the corresponding component.

Since the handle protocol is event-based, a late-joining client has no way to know the current state of the UI unless the extension explicitly sends it. That is what `onClientJoin` is for. The extension can use it to send a hydration event to the newly attached client, in case the initial data passed to `open()` isn't sufficient:

```ts
handle.onClientJoin((client) => {
  handle.send({ type: "state", todos }, { to: client.id });
});
```

If a client disconnects while a handle is open, only that client's local instance goes away. The logical handle stays open as long as at least one attached client remains. If the last attached client disappears, the handle closes automatically with reason `"no_clients"`.

## What this one primitive covers

All of the following patterns use the same `open()` API. The only difference is how the extension chooses to use events and closure.

**Question dialog.** Server opens a handle, client renders a temporary dialog, user fills it in and the client calls `close(result)`. The server awaits `handle.closed` and gets the answer.

**Persistent overlay.** Server opens a handle and keeps sending events over time to update what the client renders. Nobody has to await anything. The server can close it when it is no longer needed. The client can dismiss it locally.

**Server-driven progress.** Server opens a handle, does async work, and sends progress events. The client renders a spinner or progress bar. When the work finishes, the server closes the handle. If the user presses Escape, the client closes it and the server sees reason `"client_closed"`.

**Server-mediated shared UI across multiple clients.** Server opens one logical handle. All connected clients get local instances. Clients send events back to the server. The server keeps authoritative state, decides what those events mean, and rebroadcasts updated state to all clients. New clients joining late get hydrated via `onClientJoin`.

A small example of that last pattern:

```ts
const handle = ctx.ui.open("todos", {});

let todos: { text: string; done: boolean }[] = [];

handle.onEvent((event) => {
  if (event.type === "add") {
    todos.push({ text: event.text, done: false });
    handle.send({ type: "state", todos });
  }

  if (event.type === "toggle") {
    todos[event.index]!.done = !todos[event.index]!.done;
    handle.send({ type: "state", todos });
  }
});

handle.onClientJoin((client) => {
  handle.send({ type: "state", todos }, { to: client.id });
});
```

On the client side, the component sends events like `{ type: "add", text }` or `{ type: "toggle", index }`, and rerenders whenever it receives a `{ type: "state", todos }` event from the server.

## Local dismiss vs terminal close

`close()` is always terminal. It ends the logical handle for every client.

That is the right behavior for question dialogs and wizards. But for persistent overlays like a status panel, one user dismissing it should not kill it for everyone else.

The solution is that local dismiss is not a `close()`. The client just hides the component locally without calling `close()`. If the client wants to tell the server it dismissed the UI, it can send a regular event like `{ type: "dismiss" }`. The server can record that this client is no longer interested and stop sending it updates. That is up to the extension to implement. The handle stays open for everyone else.

So the rule is:

- `close(result?)` is terminal, ends the handle for all clients
- local dismiss is a client-side UI decision, optionally communicated as a regular event

## Other scenarios, possibly incomplete

I have thought through a few more edge cases.

**Multiple overlapping handles.** The server can open multiple handles at the same time. They are independent. Clients stack them in their overlay system. This is already covered by the design.

**Server wants to remove one client from a handle without closing it.** For example, revoking access to a shared panel for one user. The current API does not have a server-side `detach(client)`. The extension can send a targeted event like `{ type: "close" }` to that specific client and have the client hide locally. The server can then track internally that this client should no longer receive updates.

**Client wants to reopen a dismissed handle.** Since dismiss is local and the handle is still open, the client could just re-show it. This is really up to the extension implementation, and all kinds of scenarios can be covered via passing events between client and server.

**Client leaves handles on session switch/fork.** If a client switches to a different session or forks, it detaches from all handles in the old session. The handles themselves stay open on the server as long as other clients are still attached. If that was the last client, they close with `no_clients`.

# The `broadcast()` primitive

`broadcast()` is for server-to-client state sync without UI components or handles.

The use case: a server-side extension has some derived state that client-side code needs for rendering. Plan mode status, todo progress, connection indicators, custom labels. The server owns the state. The client just needs to see it.

## Server-side API

```ts
pi.broadcast(channel: string, value: unknown): void;
```

That is the entire server-side API. Call it with a channel name and a value. The server remembers the latest value per channel. All connected clients on the same session receive it. When a new client joins the session, it automatically gets the latest value for every active broadcast channel.

To clear a channel:

```ts
pi.broadcast("plan-mode", undefined);
```

## Client-side API

```ts
ui.subscribe(channel: string, handler: (value: unknown) => void): () => void;
```

The handler is called immediately with the current value if one exists, and again whenever the server broadcasts a new value. Returns an unsubscribe function.

## How it differs from `open()`

`open()` is for interactive UI with bidirectional events and close semantics. It creates a component on the client. It has lifecycle.

`broadcast()` is a reactive key-value store. The server sets values. Clients observe them. No components, no handles, no lifecycle, no bidirectional events. Just data flowing from server to clients.

## How it differs from `appendEntry()`

`appendEntry()` persists data to the session file for reconstruction on restart. It is invisible at runtime. Nothing reacts to it in real time.

`broadcast()` is live, in-memory, session-scoped state. It does not persist. On session start, the server extension reconstructs its internal state from `appendEntry()` data, then calls `broadcast()` to push the current derived display state to clients.

## Example: plan mode

Server:

```ts
let planModeEnabled = false;
let todoItems: TodoItem[] = [];

function broadcastState(): void {
  pi.broadcast("plan-mode", { planModeEnabled, executionMode, todoItems });
}

pi.on("session_start", async (_event, ctx) => {
  // restore from persisted entries
  reconstructState(ctx);
  // push current state to all clients
  broadcastState();
});

pi.on("turn_end", async (event) => {
  // update state
  markCompletedSteps(...);
  persistState();
  // push updated state
  broadcastState();
});
```

Client:

```ts
ui.subscribe("plan-mode", (state) => {
  if (state.executionMode && state.todoItems.length > 0) {
    const completed = state.todoItems.filter(t => t.completed).length;
    ui.setStatus("plan-mode", ui.theme.fg("accent", `📋 ${completed}/${state.todoItems.length}`));
    ui.setWidget("plan-todos", state.todoItems.map(renderTodoLine));
  } else if (state.planModeEnabled) {
    ui.setStatus("plan-mode", ui.theme.fg("warning", "⏸ plan"));
    ui.setWidget("plan-todos", undefined);
  } else {
    ui.setStatus("plan-mode", undefined);
    ui.setWidget("plan-todos", undefined);
  }
});
```

No fake components. No handles. No lifecycle management. The server sets the value, the client renders from it.

## Example: connection status

Server:

```ts
pi.on("agent_start", () => {
  pi.broadcast("agent-status", { running: true });
});

pi.on("agent_end", () => {
  pi.broadcast("agent-status", { running: false });
});
```

Client:

```ts
ui.subscribe("agent-status", (status) => {
  ui.setStatus("agent", status.running ? "working..." : undefined);
});
```

## Summary: three server-side state mechanisms

| Mechanism | Persists | Syncs to clients | Use case |
|---|---|---|---|
| `appendEntry()` | yes | no | State reconstruction on restart |
| `broadcast()` | no | yes, including late joiners | Driving client-side ambient UI |
| `open()` | no | yes, including late joiners | Interactive UI with bidirectional events |

# Server and transport

## Security

[GHSA-vxw4-wv6m-9hhh](https://github.com/anomalyco/opencode/security/advisories/GHSA-vxw4-wv6m-9hhh) is a good example of what not to do. OpenCode started an unauthenticated HTTP server with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`, which meant any local process and any website the user visited could execute arbitrary shell commands as the user. Three failures: no authentication, wildcard CORS allowing browser-based drive-by RCE, and an option to bind to `0.0.0.0` exposing the server to the local network.

For pi:

- bind to `127.0.0.1` by default
- never bind `0.0.0.0` unless the user explicitly opts in
- require authentication on every connection
- no permissive CORS

## Server startup

In server mode, pi starts an HTTP/WebSocket server on `127.0.0.1` with a random port (or a user-specified port). It generates a password and prints connection info to stdout.

The server also serves the built-in web harness as static files at the same origin, so a browser can connect directly without CORS.

## Connecting

Clients connect via WebSocket. The handshake includes client type (`tui`, `web`), client version, and password. The server checks the password and version compatibility. Incompatible versions are rejected.

Version checks only matter for TUI clients. Web clients are served by the server itself, so they are always compatible.

Extensions are per-session, not per-server, so they are not part of the initial handshake.

## Sessions

After connecting, the client can list, create, join, leave, and delete sessions.

A client can only be attached to one session at a time. Joining a new session implicitly leaves the previous one. Leaving means the client stops receiving events, detaches from all open UI handles, and triggers `no_clients` closure on any handles where it was the last attached client.

## Joining a session

When a client joins or creates a session, the server sends a snapshot:

- session state: model, thinking level, active tools, context usage, session name, the full conversation branch
- extensions active in this session and whether client modules exist
- all currently open UI handles
- shared session settings

The client resolves extensions locally, reports back what it could and could not load, renders the conversation from the snapshot, and mounts any replayed UI handles.

After the snapshot, the connection becomes a live bidirectional event stream.

## Server to client

The server pushes:

- agent lifecycle events (start, end, turn start/end)
- message events (start, streaming updates, end)
- tool execution events (start, partial updates, end)
- compaction and retry events
- queue updates (steering and follow-up messages)
- session settings changes (broadcast when any client changes a shared setting)
- extension UI (open, handle-scoped events, close)
- extension errors
- bash streaming (start, output chunks, completion)
- resource listings after startup and reload

## Client to server

The client sends:

- prompts, steering messages, follow-up messages
- abort
- session mutations: set model, cycle model, set thinking level, set active tools, compact, rename, navigate tree, fork, switch, reload extensions
- bash execution
- extension UI: handle-scoped events, close, unavailable errors
- data queries for selectors (session list, tree, fork points, available models)

Some commands are request/response (fork returns a snapshot). Some are fire-and-forget (abort). The protocol uses optional `requestId` fields to distinguish them.

## Local mode

When you run `pi` today without a server, that is local mode. It should be a special case of the same architecture: an in-process server and an in-process TUI client connected by direct function calls instead of WebSocket messages.

The TUI shell should not care which transport it is using. It talks to an abstract session client interface. Local mode and remote mode are just different implementations of that interface.

## Settings

Settings split into three scopes.

**Session settings** are shared across all clients on that session. When one client changes a session setting, the server broadcasts the change. Examples: model, thinking level, auto compaction, active tools, steering mode, follow-up mode, session name.

**Client-local settings** never travel over the wire. Each client manages its own. Examples: theme, hide thinking blocks, hardware cursor, editor padding, autocomplete size, local keybindings, quiet startup, collapse changelog, double-escape action, show images.

**Server settings** live on the server machine and are not session-specific. Examples: auth credentials, resource paths, model registry config.

The `/settings` UI in a client shows a mix: session-backed controls that send commands to the server, and local-only controls that update the client's own store.

# Design principle: dogfooding the extension API

The built-in TUI should be buildable with the same API that third-party extensions use. If the harness needs special powers that extensions do not have, the API is incomplete.

The TUI harness itself should be as thin as possible: terminal I/O, layout, the input editor, event stream rendering, and routing. Everything else should be expressible as client extensions using the same `TuiExtensionAPI`:

- footer (model, tokens, git branch, extension statuses)
- header (logo, keybinding hints, changelog)
- `/tree`, `/model`, `/settings`, `/new`, `/resume`, `/fork` (local selectors that send commands to the server)
- keyboard shortcuts (ctrl+p for model cycling, ctrl+o for tool expansion)
- version check and changelog notifications
- bash mode border color

The built-in "extensions" ship with the harness and use the same registration API. Third-party extensions plug in alongside them. If the API cannot express what the built-ins need, the API has a gap that needs fixing.

# Open questions

Things that are not fully resolved yet.

**Concrete extension API shapes.** The split between `ServerExtensionAPI` and `TuiExtensionAPI` is outlined but not finalized. The exact type signatures, especially for the client-side API, need to be worked out during implementation. The goal is full feature parity with the current `ExtensionAPI`.

**Server-side UI context.** We discussed removing most UI methods from the server (`setStatus`, `setWidget`, `setTitle`, etc.) because they leak client harness details. The server should ideally only have `open()`, `broadcast()`, and the convenience dialogs (`select`, `confirm`, `input`, `editor`, `notify`). But some extensions currently call these from server-side event handlers. Those need to move to client-side code that observes the same events. Whether that is always practical needs to be validated during implementation.

**Session state sync to clients.** Clients need a live view of the active branch. The session snapshot on join covers the initial state. After that, new entries need to be streamed. Message entries are already covered by the event stream. But `appendEntry()`, model changes, label changes, and other silent mutations are not currently emitted as events. The exact mechanism for keeping the client's branch view in sync needs to be defined.

**What the TUI harness owns vs what is an extension.** The line between "core harness" and "built-in extension" is not drawn precisely. Message rendering, tool execution rendering, and the streaming text display are probably core. Footer, header, built-in commands, and keyboard shortcuts are probably extensions. The exact boundary will become clear when we start building.

**Legacy extension support.** Whether old single-file extensions can keep working in local mode is still uncertain. The intent is to support them, but the compatibility boundary is not implemented.

**Web client details.** The web client API, the web harness, and how web assets are served are sketched but not designed in detail. This is deferred until the TUI client/server model works.

**Multiple clients on the same session.** The primitives support it (`open()` handles, `broadcast()` channels, session event stream). But the practical UX of two people interacting with the same session has not been thought through. For example: what happens if both clients submit a prompt at the same time? What about concurrent `/compact` and a prompt? These are runtime concerns that the current single-client `AgentSession` does not handle.

**Security beyond the basics.** The plan covers binding to `127.0.0.1`, requiring a password, and avoiding permissive CORS. But there are more questions: token rotation, TLS for non-localhost, permission scoping per client, and whether the password should be a one-time token or a persistent secret.

# Files to read before implementing

Read all of these in full before starting to scaffold the server mode.

## This plan and related docs

- `plans/plan.md` (this file)
- `plans/pi-extension-architecture.md`
- `plans/pi-ws-protocol.md`
- `plans/pi-ws-protocol-gaps.md`
- `plans/extension-rewrites/` (all files, reference implementations for new-style extensions)

## Core session and extension system

These are the files that need to become network-aware. The server wraps these. The client talks to them through the transport.

- `packages/coding-agent/src/core/agent-session.ts` (the core, owns agent lifecycle, events, compaction, retry, tool registry, extension binding)
- `packages/coding-agent/src/core/agent-session-runtime.ts` (creates runtimes, `AgentSessionRuntimeHost` handles session switching/forking)
- `packages/coding-agent/src/core/extensions/types.ts` (current `ExtensionAPI`, `ExtensionContext`, `ExtensionUIContext`, all event types, tool definition)
- `packages/coding-agent/src/core/extensions/runner.ts` (extension runner, event emission, command/shortcut dispatch)
- `packages/coding-agent/src/core/extensions/loader.ts` (extension loading from disk)
- `packages/coding-agent/src/core/session-manager.ts` (session persistence, branch tree, entries)
- `packages/coding-agent/src/core/settings-manager.ts` (settings, needs to split into server/session/client scopes)
- `packages/coding-agent/src/core/model-registry.ts` (model discovery, API key resolution)
- `packages/coding-agent/src/core/resource-loader.ts` (skills, prompts, themes, extensions discovery)
- `packages/coding-agent/src/core/sdk.ts` (programmatic session creation, `createAgentSession`)

## The current TUI (becomes the reference client)

This is what needs to be reimplemented as a thin harness plus built-in client extensions.

- `packages/coding-agent/src/modes/interactive/interactive-mode.ts` (4600 lines, the entire current TUI)
- `packages/coding-agent/src/modes/interactive/theme/theme.ts` (theme system, client-local)
- `packages/coding-agent/src/modes/interactive/components/footer.ts` (should become a built-in client extension)
- `packages/coding-agent/src/modes/interactive/components/tool-execution.ts` (tool call/result rendering)
- `packages/coding-agent/src/modes/interactive/components/assistant-message.ts` (message rendering)
- `packages/coding-agent/src/modes/interactive/components/tree-selector.ts` (built-in command UI)
- `packages/coding-agent/src/modes/interactive/components/session-selector.ts` (built-in command UI)
- `packages/coding-agent/src/modes/interactive/components/model-selector.ts` (built-in command UI)
- `packages/coding-agent/src/modes/interactive/components/settings-selector.ts` (built-in command UI)
- `packages/coding-agent/src/modes/interactive/components/custom-editor.ts` (editor with app keybindings)

## Other modes (for reference)

- `packages/coding-agent/src/modes/rpc/rpc-mode.ts` (existing RPC mode, shows how a non-TUI mode talks to AgentSession)
- `packages/coding-agent/src/modes/rpc/rpc-types.ts` (RPC protocol types)
- `packages/coding-agent/src/modes/print-mode.ts` (simplest mode, single-shot)

## Agent core (upstream dependency)

- `packages/agent/src/agent.ts` (the `Agent` class that `AgentSession` wraps)

## Extension examples (migration targets)

- `packages/coding-agent/examples/extensions/plan-mode/` (complex, needs server + client split)
- `packages/coding-agent/examples/extensions/status-line.ts` (becomes client-only)
- `packages/coding-agent/examples/extensions/permission-gate.ts` (becomes server-only)
- `packages/coding-agent/examples/extensions/todo.ts` (needs server + client split, has custom rendering)
- `packages/coding-agent/examples/extensions/question.ts` (needs server + client split, has custom UI)
- `packages/coding-agent/examples/extensions/questionnaire.ts` (complex custom UI)
- `packages/coding-agent/examples/extensions/snake.ts` (game, pure client)

